From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stage C <stage-c@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 23 Nov 2026 06:30:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] hoist loop-invariant field reads with a capture summary

The fourth increment dropped the loop-field hoist because a local borrowed
before the loop was treated as escaped for the whole function. The useful
borrows are the ones a callee does not retain: `RawVec::grow_one(&mut self)`
is generic, so its MIR is available, and it changes the vector but does not
keep the address. This patch adds an interprocedural non-capture summary and
uses it to admit those borrows to the hoist.

The summary is per function argument. An argument is *not captured* when the
callee does not store its address (or an address derived from it by a
projection, reference/raw-pointer cast, reborrow or pointer arithmetic) in
anything that outlives the call, does not return it, does not pass it to a
capturing callee, and does not turn it into an integer. Values loaded through
the pointer are data, not derived addresses; the first store of a pointer
that aliases the caller local is itself a capture and is rejected where it is
created. Unknown callees, shims, virtual calls, unclassified intrinsics,
pointer-to-integer casts, inline asm operands, unions, closure/coroutine
captures, drop types and recursive cycles are capturing. `const_eval_select`
is classified as a call to its runtime branch.

The hoist keeps its earlier local rules (nested loop only, unique preheader,
Freeze base, no in-loop write or borrow, initialized before the preheader)
and replaces the old whole-function raw-pointer bail-out: a base is admitted
only when every borrow of it reaches a call whose summary marks that argument
non-capturing and no borrow of it is used inside the loop.

The pass is opt-in through `-Zmir-capture-hoist`; the compiler, the sysroot
and the default benchmark builds do not pass it. It is a local CogWasm
experiment, not an upstream contribution.

---
